---
/**
 * Atlas Handbook landing page (ATLAS-1215 / KAN-411).
 *
 * The reference section of the publication: what the layers claim, what the
 * evidence is, what the words mean. It opens with orientation in plain language
 * and keeps the technical gateway last, because a reader arriving from a map
 * needs to know what they are looking at before they need to know how it builds.
 */
import PortalLayout from '../../../layouts/PortalLayout.astro';
import { loadHandbook, loadHandbookCoverage } from '../../../lib/handbook-content';
import { HANDBOOK_ROOT } from '../../../lib/handbook';

const projection = await loadHandbook();
const coverage = await loadHandbookCoverage();
const documented = coverage.rows.filter((row) => row.documented);

const sections = [
  {
    title: 'Layer catalogue',
    href: `${HANDBOOK_ROOT}layers/`,
    blurb: 'Every documented layer, what it claims and what it rests on.',
    count: documented.length,
  },
  {
    title: 'Methods',
    href: `${HANDBOOK_ROOT}methods/`,
    blurb: 'How a reconstruction was made, written once and cited by the layers that use it.',
    count: projection.docs.filter((d) => d.doc.docType === 'method').length,
  },
  {
    title: 'Sources and evidence',
    href: `${HANDBOOK_ROOT}evidence/`,
    blurb: 'Ledgers of instruments, witnesses and authorities behind the source-heavy layers.',
    count: projection.docs.filter((d) => d.doc.docType === 'evidence').length,
  },
  {
    title: 'Glossary',
    href: `${HANDBOOK_ROOT}glossary/`,
    blurb: 'The words this atlas uses about evidence and uncertainty, and what each commits to.',
    count: projection.docs.filter((d) => d.doc.docType === 'glossary').length,
  },
  {
    title: 'Data fields',
    href: `${HANDBOOK_ROOT}data-fields/`,
    blurb: 'Field meanings and controlled vocabularies, for reading the map and reusing the data.',
    count: projection.docs.filter((d) => d.doc.docType === 'data-fields').length,
  },
  {
    title: 'Editorial decisions',
    href: `${HANDBOOK_ROOT}decisions/`,
    blurb: 'Choices that shape what you see, and the reasoning behind them.',
    count: projection.docs.filter((d) => d.doc.docType === 'editorial-decision').length,
  },
  {
    title: 'Technical and reproducibility',
    href: `${HANDBOOK_ROOT}technical/`,
    blurb: 'Pipelines, schemas and release manifests. Not needed to read the atlas.',
    count: projection.docs.filter((d) => d.doc.docType === 'technical').length,
  },
];
---

<PortalLayout
  title="Atlas Handbook"
  description="What each layer of the Atlas claims, what evidence it rests on, and how much of it was reconstructed."
>
  <main class="shell handbook-home">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Atlas reference</p>
      <h1>Atlas Handbook</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every layer on this atlas makes a claim, and no claim is self-evident from a coloured line.
        The Handbook is where each one is stated in plain language: what you are looking at, what it
        rests on, how much of it was drawn rather than found, and what may be cited.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing here requires knowing how the site is built. The repository stays reachable from
        every page for anyone reproducing the work, and it is never the explanation.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="how">
      <h2 id="how">How to read the Atlas</h2>
      <p>
        Three distinctions carry most of the weight. A <strong>context</strong> layer frames the map and
        asserts nothing about the past - present-day borders are context, and drawing them over historical
        material is an anachronism the atlas labels rather than hides. A
        <strong>historical</strong> layer is a reconstruction of a past state, and is the only kind of
        layer that can be cited as a claim about what was there. An <strong>evidence</strong> layer describes
        a source: a depicted extent tells you what a map shows, not what existed.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a line was drawn by this project rather than taken from a survey, the layer says so
        and the styling shows it. See the <a href={`${HANDBOOK_ROOT}glossary/`}>glossary</a> for the vocabulary
        of confidence, provenance and review.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sections">
      <h2 id="sections">Sections</h2>
      <ul class="cards">
        {
          sections.map((section) => (
            <li>
              <h3>
                <a href={section.href}>{section.title}</a>
              </h3>
              <p>{section.blurb}</p>
              <p class="count">
                {section.count} {section.count === 1 ? 'record' : 'records'}
              </p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="coverage">
      <h2 id="coverage">What is documented so far</h2>
      <p>
        {documented.length} of {coverage.rows.length} published layers have a public record. The remainder
        are being migrated programme by programme; until then their evidence lives in the repository,
        which is exactly the situation this Handbook exists to end. The current gap list is published
        as <a href="/data/handbook-coverage.json">machine-readable coverage</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</PortalLayout>

<style>
  .handbook-home {
    padding-block: 2rem 4rem;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--muted, #8a8171);
  }
  h1 {
    margin-block: 0.25rem 1rem;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }
  p {
    max-width: var(--content-max);
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
  section {
    margin-block: 2.5rem;
  }
  .cards {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
  }
  @media (min-width: 45rem) {
    .cards {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    }
  }
  .cards li {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule, #3a352b);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
  }
  .cards h3 {
    margin-block: 0 0.4rem;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }
  .cards p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }
  .count {
    margin-top: 0.5rem !important;
    font-size: 0.75rem !important;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--muted, #8a8171);
  }
</style>
